Here is a function in JavaScript that checks if any of the numbers in an array is a valid prime number:
```javascript
function checkPrime(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    let isPrime = true;
    for (let j = 2; j < arr[i]; j++) {
      if (arr[i] % j === 0) {
        isPrime = false;
        break;
      }
    }
    if (isPrime) {
      console.log(arr[i] + " is a prime number.");
    }
  }
}
```
This function iterates over each element in the array and uses a nested loop to determine if the number is prime or not. It does this by checking if the number is divisible by any number between 2 and itself (excluding itself). If no number divides it evenly, then the number is considered prime and its value is printed to the console.ЎыџNЎыџNЎыџNassistant

