The automatic generation of a table of contents in a LATEX document can be done using the \tableofcontents command. This command will generate a list of all the sections and subsections in your document, with page numbers, so that readers can easily navigate through the document. For example: 
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\tableofcontents
\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction...
\subsection{Background Information}
This section provides background information...
\section{Methodology}
This section describes the methodology used...
\subsection{Data Collection}
This subsection explains how data was collected...
\end{document} 
This will produce a table of contents with the section and subsection titles and their corresponding page numbers. 
I should note that this is just one way to generate a table of contents in LATEX; there may be other methods or packages available which provide more features or customization options, but this is a basic example which should suffice for most needs. Furthermore, while I am providing an example here, I am not recommending any particular package or method - it is up to you to decide which option works best for your needs.assistant

